Port configuration for a MINIMUM ROM level without stdio files

Six changes with NO capability content, merged into one patch because they are
build configuration rather than findings. The twelve capability patches stay
separate: each of those is a measured claim about what the model does not
express, and they are counted, retired and removal-tested individually.

Each original rationale is kept below, in the order its hunk appears. They were
patches 0016 to 0021.


========================================================================
1. sys-honor-explicit-exception-stream-without-stdfiles
========================================================================
Honor an explicit sys.print_exception stream without sys stdfiles.

mp_sys_print_exception only used its optional stream argument when both MICROPY_PY_IO and
MICROPY_PY_SYS_STDFILES were enabled. A small embedded port can provide StringIO while deliberately
omitting the global stdin/stdout/stderr objects, yet the explicit stream remains fully usable.

Gate explicit stream handling only on MICROPY_PY_IO. Keep the platform printer as the one-argument
fallback when sys stdfiles are absent. This restores emergency-exception and heap-locked traceback
capture without requiring console stream objects in the Capstone domain port.


========================================================================
2. builtins-honor-explicit-print-stream-without-stdfiles
========================================================================
Honor an explicit print stream without sys stdfiles.

The file keyword of print was only available when both MICROPY_PY_IO and
MICROPY_PY_SYS_STDFILES were enabled. A small embedded port can provide writable stream objects
while deliberately omitting the global stdin/stdout/stderr objects, just like the explicit stream
case for sys.print_exception.

Expose the file keyword whenever MICROPY_PY_IO is enabled. Use a NULL default to retain the
platform printer when sys stdfiles are absent, and otherwise route all output through either that
printer or the explicitly supplied stream.


========================================================================
3. import-resolve-built-in-subpackages-without-external-import
========================================================================
Resolve built-in subpackages without external import support.

The reduced importer used when MICROPY_ENABLE_EXTERNAL_IMPORT is disabled only looked up the full
requested name in the built-in module tables. It therefore could not import a built-in subpackage
such as string.templatelib, even when MICROPY_MODULE_BUILTIN_SUBPACKAGES was enabled and the parent
module exposed that submodule in its fixed globals.

Walk dotted names through built-in modules in the reduced importer, using the same fixed-globals
contract as the external importer. Preserve Python's top-level-versus-leaf return rule according
to whether a from-list was supplied.


========================================================================
4. float-omit-module-constants-without-math-modules
========================================================================
Omit module-only float constants when math modules are disabled.

The e and pi singleton objects are only referenced by the math and cmath module tables. A port
that enables core float objects while omitting both modules should not retain those otherwise
unreachable globals. Besides saving data on small ports, this avoids unnecessary global-capability
initialisation entries on Capstone.


========================================================================
5. deflate-honor-the-port-stream-ioctl-argument-type
========================================================================
Give deflate's stream ioctl the port-configurable argument type.

Patch 0012 made MICROPY_STREAM_IOCTL_ARG_TYPE configurable because uintptr_t keeps only the
address of a 128-bit Capstone capability, and converted every ioctl slot in py/ to the new
mp_stream_ioctl_arg_t. extmod/moddeflate.c was outside that sweep and still declares uintptr_t,
so on a port that selects a different carrier the initialiser of deflateio_stream_p is an
incompatible function pointer -- a diagnostic upstream, and a wrong call ABI in practice.

deflateio_ioctl reads only `request`, never `arg`, so this is the declaration alone.


========================================================================
6. vfs-fat-file-honor-the-port-stream-ioctl-argument-type
========================================================================
Give the FAT file object's stream ioctl the port-configurable argument type.

Patch 0012 made MICROPY_STREAM_IOCTL_ARG_TYPE configurable because uintptr_t keeps only the
address of a 128-bit Capstone capability, and converted every ioctl slot in py/ to the new
mp_stream_ioctl_arg_t. Patch 0020 did the same for extmod/moddeflate.c. extmod/vfs_fat_file.c
was outside both sweeps; it is only reached when MICROPY_VFS is on, which is why this surfaced
now rather than then.

Two changes, and the second is the one that matters. file_obj_ioctl, unlike deflateio_ioctl,
actually USES arg: it casts it to a struct mp_stream_seek_t *, and it does so through
(uintptr_t), which on this target strips the tag and hands MP_STREAM_SEEK an untagged word.
That is not a diagnostic, it is a capability fault at the first dereference. With arg already
carrying the capability the intermediate cast is not merely unnecessary, it is wrong.

